  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1225 PM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nPWATs are modeled to peak Wednesday and challenge calendar day \nrecords. Wednesday will be one of the wettest days in recent \nmemory, in terms of widespread soaking rainfall. The 12Z NAM MOS \nis showing a 100 PoP for KABQ on Wednesday, which is extremely \nrare for our area. The day will start with ongoing precipitation \nand a rain-cooled lower boundary layer that will significantly \nlimit instability, but a mid level disturbance is forecast to move\nnorth over western NM and provide added forcing to keep some \nconvection going. Highs on Wednesday will be well below average \nthanks to the rain-cooling and cloud cover. The disturbance will \nshift northeast across northern NM Wednesday night, refocusing \nheavier rainfall further north. The flash flood risk will increase\nWednesday over a larger area and the current Flash Flood Watch \ngoes through Wednesday at 6PM, but may need to be extended through\nThursday. The atmosphere will be worked-over going into Thursday \nand will take awhile to destabilize and produce a crop of \nconvection absent a mid level disturbance like Wednesday. The \n3-day storm total mean qpf is impressive, favoring central and \neastern NM with amounts generally ranging from 2-4\". The burn scar\nflood threat in Ruidoso will be high on Wed/Thu, with increasingly\nwet antecedent conditions requiring less rainfall to produce \nflash flooding. The Pacific low/trough is forecast to open up and \nprogress east across the southern Rockies and NM Friday, producing\na crop of convection across our area despite a downtrend in \nPWATs. Grounds will be saturated by Friday, so the flash flood \nthreat will persist with less rain needed to cause problems. PWATs\nwill continue the downtrend over the weekend with warming under \nthe influence of the westerlies. A low amplitude upper level \ntrough is forecast to progress eastward across the Intermountain \nWest late Sunday through Monday, interacting with lef-over \nmoisture across NM and producing more showers and storms.",
